CAT = "sqeContract"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("A seller promises to hold her offer open for a week, but on day three tells the buyer she has sold elsewhere. Can he still accept?",
 "No, it was revoked", ["Yes, the week has not run", "Yes, her promise binds her", "Only if he relied on it"],
 "An offer can be revoked at any time before acceptance if the revocation is communicated, even by a reliable third party; a promise to keep it open is unenforceable without consideration (Routledge v Grant, Dickinson v Dodds)."),
("A seller posts a letter accepting an offer, properly addressed, which arrives late. When was the contract made?",
 "When it was posted", ["When it was read", "When it was delivered", "When the buyer replied"],
 "Under the postal rule, acceptance by post is effective on posting where post is a reasonable means of acceptance and the rule is not excluded."),
("A buyer replies to an offer by proposing a lower price. What is the effect on the original offer?",
 "It is rejected", ["It is accepted", "It stays open", "It becomes irrevocable"],
 "A counter-offer rejects the original offer, which cannot then be accepted (Hyde v Wrench)."),
("After a builder finishes work, the owner promises him a bonus. Can the builder enforce it?",
 "No, it is past consideration", ["Yes, as a firm promise", "Yes, if made in writing", "No, it lacks certainty"],
 "Consideration given before the promise is past consideration and is no good. The exception needs the act to be done at the promisor's request with payment understood."),
("A landlord agrees to take half rent during a crisis and the tenant relies on it. What protects the tenant?",
 "Promissory estoppel", ["Economic duress", "Frustration", "A collateral contract"],
 "Promissory estoppel stops the promisor going back on a clear promise not to enforce strict rights where the other party relied on it, at least for the period of reliance."),
("A seller carelessly misstated a car's mileage, inducing the sale. How are damages measured under the 1967 Act?",
 "As if it were fraud", ["Expectation loss only", "Nominal sum only", "Half the price paid"],
 "Damages under s.2(1) Misrepresentation Act 1967 are assessed on the tort of deceit measure, so all direct loss is recoverable even if unforeseeable (Royscot Trust v Rogerson)."),
("A business contract tries to exclude liability for death caused by negligence. What is its effect?",
 "None, it is void", ["Valid if reasonable", "Valid if signed", "Valid if in bold"],
 "Liability for death or personal injury from negligence cannot be excluded: s.2(1) UCTA 1977 for business contracts and s.65 Consumer Rights Act 2015 for consumer contracts."),
("A trader signs a written contract without reading a clause limiting the seller's liability. Is the clause incorporated?",
 "Yes, by his signature", ["No, he never read it", "Only if it was explained", "Only if it is printed boldly"],
 "A person who signs a contractual document is bound by its terms whether or not they read them, absent fraud or misrepresentation (L'Estrange v Graucob)."),
("Breach of which type of term always allows the innocent party to terminate?",
 "Condition", ["Warranty", "Innominate term", "Representation"],
 "Breach of a condition entitles the innocent party to treat the contract as ended. Breach of a warranty gives damages only; for an innominate term it depends on the seriousness of the breach."),
("A hired hall burns down before the event, through no one's fault. What is the position?",
 "Contract is frustrated", ["Hirer must still pay", "Owner must find a new hall", "Contract is voidable"],
 "Where performance becomes impossible through a supervening event without either party's fault, the contract is frustrated and both are discharged (Taylor v Caldwell)."),
("After a contract is frustrated, what happens to a deposit already paid?",
 "Recoverable, less expenses", ["Kept by the payee in full", "Forfeit to the payee", "Repaid with damages added"],
 "Under s.1(2) Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 money paid is recoverable, but the court may let the payee keep expenses incurred before discharge."),
("A carrier delays delivery; the buyer loses an unusually lucrative deal the carrier was never told about. Can the buyer recover it?",
 "No, it is too remote", ["Yes, the delay caused it", "Yes, all losses count", "Only if he mitigated"],
 "Under Hadley v Baxendale only losses arising naturally, or within the parties' reasonable contemplation when contracting, are recoverable. Special losses need special knowledge."),
("A buyer could have bought substitute goods at once but waited while prices rose. Effect on damages?",
 "Avoidable loss is lost", ["Full loss is recovered", "The contract becomes void", "Seller pays twice the loss"],
 "The claimant must take reasonable steps to mitigate. Damages exclude loss that reasonable steps would have avoided."),
("A contract expressly gives a benefit to a named third party. Can the third party enforce that term?",
 "Yes, under the 1999 Act", ["No, privity prevents it", "Only on assignment", "Only if it is a deed"],
 "The Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 lets an identified third party enforce a term that expressly confers a benefit on them, unless the parties intended otherwise."),
("A contract fixes a sum payable for late completion. When will a court refuse to enforce it?",
 "If out of all proportion", ["If the actual loss is smaller", "If not made by deed", "If the delay was brief"],
 "After Cavendish v Makdessi a clause is a penalty only if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest in performance."),
("A consumer's new washing machine stops working after two weeks. What remedy can she use?",
 "Short-term right to reject", ["Damages for distress", "A repair at her own cost", "A store credit only"],
 "Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 goods must be of satisfactory quality; within 30 days a consumer can use the short-term right to reject for a full refund."),
]
